{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>Behold, I am going to die.</b> He agreed to Jacob's words, for it is evident that Jacob cooked the stew with intention and not by chance, for he knew that Esau would come from the field and his desire was to acquire the birthright from him, and to press upon him: 'Behold, you go every day to a place of bands of wild beasts and your life hangs suspended before you — and why do you need the birthright? For in my eyes you are counted as dead every day.' And therefore he made a dish of lentils, which is what is prepared to comfort the mourner, along the lines of what Rashi explained on the verse (Genesis 27:42) ״מִתְנַחֵם לְךָ לְהָרְגֶךָ״ — 'he has already drunk the cup of consolation over you.' So too did Jacob make the meal of recovery for Esau, to say to him: 'Behold, you are regarded in my eyes as though you were already dead — and why do you need the birthright?' And Esau acknowledged his words and said, 'Behold, I am going to die' — and I am closer to death than to life; if so, why do I need the birthright? In the word ״זֶה לִי״, he is as it were pointing with his finger at the matter to which the birthright pertains even on that day, and this is the service that belonged to the firstborn."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:14.123152780Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}